\begin{abstract}
This document presents a complete BitTorrent client implemented as a literate
program. The narrative explains the BitTorrent protocol from first principles
while the code chunks build a working implementation capable of downloading
real torrents. The source is organized to follow the logical flow of the
protocol rather than module boundaries, making it suitable for learning both
the protocol and literate programming methodology.
\end{abstract}

\tableofcontents

\section{Introduction}

BitTorrent is a peer-to-peer file sharing protocol designed for efficient
distribution of large files. Unlike traditional client-server downloads where
a single server bears the entire load, BitTorrent distributes the burden among
all participants: each peer both downloads and uploads pieces of the file.

This document implements a BitTorrent client from scratch. We will cover:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Bencode}: The encoding format used for torrent metadata
\item \textbf{Metainfo files}: The structure of \texttt{.torrent} files
\item \textbf{Tracker protocol}: How peers discover each other
\item \textbf{Peer wire protocol}: How peers exchange data
\item \textbf{Piece management}: Tracking and verifying downloaded content
\item \textbf{Download strategy}: Choosing what to request from whom
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Document Roadmap}

The implementation follows the data flow of a typical download:

\begin{enumerate}
\item Parse the torrent file (requires understanding Bencode)
\item Contact the tracker to get a list of peers
\item Connect to peers and negotiate what pieces they have
\item Request and receive blocks, assembling them into pieces
\item Verify piece integrity via SHA1 hashes
\item Write completed pieces to disk
\end{enumerate}

